.TH "GIT-FOREST" "1" "2026-10-11" "git-forest" "Git Manual"
.SH NAME
git-forest \- manage named workspaces across multiple Git repositories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git forest
.RI [ options ]
.I command
.br
.B git-forest
.RI [ options ]
.I command
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git-forest
manages one named workspace across multiple configured canonical repositories
using linked Git worktrees.
Git exposes the installed executable as both
.B git-forest
and
.BR "git forest" .
.PP
Without a command, Forest prints its command-line help.
.PP
Forest treats Git worktree metadata and the filesystem as authoritative.
It does not maintain a separate registry, reset or delete branches, start
runtime services, or perform implicit network operations.
Only
.BR setup ,
.BR fetch ,
and
.B update
contact remotes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display help.
When using the Git-style command, Git handles
.B git forest \-\-help
itself by opening this manual page.
Use
.B git forest \-h
for concise command-line help.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Display the git-forest version.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Use an explicit configuration file instead of configuration discovery.
This option is global and may be used with any command.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit structured JSON.
This option is available on every command except
.BR completions .
Application errors are then emitted as JSON on standard error.
The report formats are documented in
.B docs/json.md
in the git-forest repository.
.PP
Human output is colored only when standard output is a terminal.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B setup
Ensure configured canonical repositories are cloned.
Existing Git worktrees are reused without fetching or changing their remotes.
Missing repositories are cloned from the rendered
.B repositories.remote
template in configuration order.
.IP
Before cloning, Forest checks every configured destination.
An existing path that is not a Git worktree, or a missing repository without a
configured remote, prevents all cloning.
If a clone fails after earlier repositories succeeded, the successful clones
are preserved and later repositories are not run; repeating the command reuses
completed clones and resumes the rest.
Clones are completed in adjacent staging directories before being moved into
place, so an interrupted clone is never reused as a completed repository.
Forest never overwrites an existing path.
.TP
.B repos
List configured repositories in configuration order, with their origin URLs
and locally known default refs.
Missing canonical clones are reported rather than failing the command.
The default ref is read only from the symbolic ref
.BR refs/remotes/origin/HEAD .
Forest never guesses
.B main
or
.B master
and never contacts a remote to repair a missing default.
.TP
.BI fetch " [repository ...] [\-\-jobs N]"
Fetch
.B origin
for all configured repositories or for the named subset.
Fetches run concurrently, at most 16 at a time unless
.BI \-\-jobs " N"
sets another bound.
The report stays in configuration order.
Every selected repository is attempted, and the command exits unsuccessfully if
any fetch fails.
Tags are not fetched because workspace operations use only remote-tracking
branch refs.
.IP
Fetching updates remote-tracking refs, including the
.B origin/HEAD
target used as the default creation base, but never merges, resets, or
otherwise updates local branches or worktrees.
.TP
.BI update " [repository ...] [\-\-jobs N]"
Fetch and fast-forward canonical default branches for all configured
repositories or for the named subset.
Fetches use the same bounded concurrency as
.BR fetch ,
but transfer only the branch identified by
.BR refs/remotes/origin/HEAD .
.IP
If the branch is checked out, Forest updates its worktree with a
fast-forward-only merge.
If it is not, Forest moves the local ref only after verifying a fast-forward.
Dirty checked-out branches, ignored paths that overlap incoming changes,
missing local default branches, locally ahead branches, and diverged branches
are reported as conflicts and are never reset or forced.
A failure in one repository does not stop the others, but conflicts and
failures make the command exit unsuccessfully.
.TP
.BI create " workspace [checkout ...] [\-\-symbol symbol] [\-\-parent workspace] [\-\-base checkout=ref ...] [\-\-branch checkout=branch ...]"
Create a workspace, optionally with linked worktrees.
When no checkouts are provided, create an empty workspace directory so
worktrees can be added later with
.BR add ;
repeating empty creation is safe.
Archived names remain available: creation starts a fresh active workspace and
leaves saved files untouched, though existing branches may be reused.
Use
.B unarchive
to restore saved workspace files.
.IP
A checkout is
.B repository
for its primary worktree or
.B repository@slot
for an additional worktree from the same repository.
A primary checkout uses the repository name as its directory, such as
.BR stacked/api ;
a named checkout uses
.BR stacked/api@part-2 .
The default branch of a named checkout renders the slot as
.BR {checkout} ,
so with the template
.B user/{checkout}
the command
.B create stacked api operator operator@part-2
creates
.B user/stacked
at
.B api
and
.BR operator ,
and
.B user/part-2
at
.BR operator@part-2 .
Checkout identifiers must be unique within a request, and two checkouts from
the same repository may not select the same branch.
.IP
Before mutation, every requested checkout is checked for:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
a present canonical Git worktree;
.IP \(bu 2
a valid rendered branch name;
.IP \(bu 2
a non-conflicting destination path;
.IP \(bu 2
existing worktree registration;
.IP \(bu 2
branches checked out elsewhere;
.IP \(bu 2
branch namespace conflicts;
.IP \(bu 2
a resolvable base when a new branch is needed.
.RE
.IP
Preflight conflicts prevent all mutation.
If Git fails after earlier checkouts have been created, successful worktrees
are preserved and later checkouts are marked as not run; repeating the command
resumes.
.IP
An existing worktree is reused only when its path, canonical repository, and
branch all match.
An existing branch that is not checked out elsewhere is added without being
recreated.
New branches start from
.B origin/HEAD
unless
.BI \-\-base " checkout=ref"
is given.
.IP
.BI \-\-branch " checkout=branch"
selects a branch independently of the configured template.
Forest first uses an existing local branch.
If it is absent, Forest creates a local branch from
.BI refs/remotes/origin/ branch
and configures the remote branch as its upstream.
The remote-tracking ref must already exist locally; creation never fetches.
A branch override and a base override cannot both target the same checkout.
After later fetches, Forest reports whether a tracking branch is behind but
never merges, resets, or otherwise updates it.
.IP
.BI \-\-symbol " symbol"
saves an emoji or symbol that
.B list
shows beside the workspace name, and
.BI \-\-parent " workspace"
records an active workspace as this workspace's parent.
Parents may nest to any depth; a parent that would form a cycle is rejected.
Workspace directories stay side by side under the workspaces root; the link is
metadata only.
Both values are stored in the optional workspace-local
.B .forest-workspace.toml
file as
.B symbol
and
.BR parent .
The file is authoritative, is read on every command, may be edited by hand, and
is preserved across rename, archive, and unarchive.
When Forest writes it, other keys are kept but comments are not.
An invalid file is reported with its path.
The file appears in workspace entries like other workspace-local files.
Symbols must be nonempty and contain no whitespace or control characters;
composed emoji are supported.
Repeating creation with either option updates that value; omitting it preserves
the saved value.
Directory names, branch names, and JSON workspace identifiers are unaffected.
A legacy
.B .forest-symbol
file is still read, ignoring trailing whitespace, and is replaced the next time
Forest writes metadata.
.TP
.BI add " workspace checkout ... [\-\-base checkout=ref ...] [\-\-branch checkout=branch ...]"
Add primary or named linked worktrees to an existing workspace.
At least one checkout is required.
Checkouts are preflighted and created as for
.BR create .
.TP
.B list [\-\-archived]
List active workspaces by reconciling workspace directories with every
canonical repository's Git worktree metadata.
Human output is a tree of workspace names, each under its parent and prefixed
with its saved symbol, or an evergreen tree emoji when it has none.
JSON keeps a flat list and reports each
.B parent
and
.BR symbol ,
primary and named checkouts, unregistered paths, missing registered paths,
workspace-local entries, and layout mismatches.
Workspace-local entries are direct children of the workspace that are not
configured checkout paths; they are supported and do not make the workspace
inconsistent.
Their JSON field is named
.B unexpected_entries
for compatibility.
.IP
With
.BR \-\-archived ,
list archives instead, with workspace name, human-readable date, archive ID,
and path.
Legacy archives show
.B legacy
instead of a date.
JSON output uses an
.B archives
array containing
.BR workspace ,
.BR archive_id ,
and
.B archive_path
fields rather than the active
.B workspaces
array.
.TP
.BI status " [workspace]"
Show what
.B list
reports in JSON, and for each checkout its current branch or detached state,
HEAD commit, tracked and untracked dirty state, canonical worktree
registration, upstream, and ahead and behind counts.
.TP
.BI path " workspace"
Print the absolute path of an existing workspace.
Human output is exactly the path followed by a newline.
.TP
.BI rename " workspace new-workspace"
Rename an active workspace without changing its branches, commits, upstreams,
or working tree contents.
Forest preflights the complete workspace, atomically moves its directory, and
uses
.B git worktree repair
to update worktree metadata in each canonical repository.
Dirty, untracked, and ignored files and workspace-local entries are preserved.
An inconsistent source or active destination prevents the rename.
Archives do not reserve names and remain untouched.
Rename never fetches and has no
.B \-\-force
option.
.IP
Existing branch names remain unchanged.
If the branch template uses
.BR {workspace} ,
a checkout created after the rename uses the new workspace name while existing
checkouts keep branches rendered with the old name.
Forest cannot tell which branches came from the template rather than an
explicit
.B \-\-branch
override.
.IP
A Git repair failure can leave the directory at the new path with
registrations still pointing to the old path; repeating the same command
recognizes that state and resumes the repair.
Before moving anything, rename updates
.B parent
in each active child, restoring it if the directory move fails.
A resumed rename updates any children that still name the old workspace.
.TP
.BI archive " workspace [\-\-force]"
Remove every clean registered worktree in a workspace with
.BR "git worktree remove" ,
preserve its branches, and, once every removal succeeds, atomically move its
workspace-local entries to a new generation beneath
.BR workspaces.root/.archive/.generations .
For example:
.BR .archive/.generations/nkdb-azure\-\-2026-09-26-09-46-11-0700 .
IDs use local time and its UTC offset in YYYY-MM-DD-HH-MM-SS\(+-HHMM form;
same-second collisions append -2, -3, and so on.
Older generations remain untouched.
Legacy
.B .archive/workspace
directories remain supported with ID
.BR legacy .
The separate
.B .generations
directory disambiguates timestamp-looking legacy workspace names.
There is no manifest or database.
Within a workspace, archives are ordered by their actual instant and numeric
collision suffix, with legacy archives first.
.IP
Dirty worktrees, unregistered checkout paths, layout mismatches, a missing
workspace directory, and active child workspaces prevent archival;
workspace-local entries do not.
Archive or delete the children first, or point them at another parent.
Archival never overwrites files and is safe to repeat after partial execution.
Use
.B \-\-force
only to discard modified, untracked, or ignored worktree files; all other
conflicts still prevent archival.
Repeating a successful archive reports it as already archived while no new
active workspace exists, selecting the newest generation or the legacy archive
if it is the only one.
.IP
Archives are excluded from the active workspace list, active workspace
completion, and
.BR path ;
use
.B list \-\-archived
to see them.
The archive is local dormant storage, not a compressed archive or backup.
.TP
.BI unarchive " workspace [checkout ...] [\-\-archive ID] [\-\-as workspace]"
Restore saved workspace-local files, optionally creating only the requested
checkouts; previous checkouts are not recreated automatically.
It accepts the same
.B \-\-base
and
.B \-\-branch
options as
.BR create .
Use
.BI \-\-symbol " symbol"
and
.BI \-\-parent " workspace"
to override the saved symbol or parent when restoring; otherwise they are
preserved.
A single archive is selected automatically; multiple archives require
.BR "\-\-archive ID" .
Use ID
.B legacy
for an older archive, and
.B \-\-as
to restore under a different active workspace name.
Forest preflights all requested checkouts before moving files, refuses an
existing active destination, and never overwrites files.
Restoration consumes the selected archive and leaves other generations
untouched.
.IP
If checkout creation fails after the move, repeat the command.
When no matching archive remains and the destination is active,
Forest reconciles requested checkouts and reports
.BR already_active .
It never merges an archive into an active workspace.
Without a manifest, it cannot verify the workspace's origin or distinguish a
well-formed nonexistent ID from a consumed one.
Malformed IDs are rejected before reconciliation.
Invalid IDs and ambiguous selections return conflict reports.
.TP
.BI delete " workspace [\-\-force]"
Permanently delete a workspace while preserving its Git branches.
Forest first applies the same complete-workspace preflight and worktree removal
as
.BR archive ,
then stages workspace-local files at
.B .archive/.deleting/workspace
and permanently removes them.
Pending deletions are excluded from archive discovery and cannot be restored
with
.BR unarchive .
Dirty or unregistered worktrees and active child workspaces prevent deletion.
Use
.B \-\-force
only to discard modified, untracked, or ignored files in registered worktrees;
it does not delete branches or bypass unregistered-path checks.
Deleting an active workspace preserves older archives of that name.
.IP
If removal fails after staging, the report is
.BR failed ;
repeat the command to remove the pending files.
If a new active workspace has reused the name, retry refuses both paths until
you rename the active workspace.
With no active workspace, remaining registrations, or pending deletion, the
workspace is reported as already deleted.
Deletion never falls back to retained archives.
To delete an archive, including a legacy archive, restore it with
.B unarchive \-\-archive ID
(optionally using
.BR \-\-as )
and then delete the restored workspace.
.TP
.B clean
Remove stale Git registrations for Forest worktrees whose directories were
deleted outside Forest.
Forest scans every configured canonical repository and uses
.B git worktree remove
for registrations beneath the active workspace root whose paths no longer
exist.
It preserves branches, ignores present worktrees, and does not affect stale
registrations outside the configured workspace root or beneath
.BR .archive .
Every stale registration is attempted, and the command exits unsuccessfully if
any removal fails.
It is safe to repeat and succeeds with an empty report when nothing needs
cleaning.
Use
.B remove
or
.B archive
for normal retirement so Forest can reject dirty worktrees;
.B clean
cannot recover deleted contents.
.TP
.BI remove " workspace [checkout ...] [\-\-force]"
Remove all worktrees in a workspace or only the named checkouts.
Removal rejects dirty paths unless
.B \-\-force
is passed, which discards modified, untracked, and ignored files.
Every selected path must be registered with its configured canonical
repository, even with
.BR \-\-force .
Removal uses
.B git worktree remove
for present worktrees and stale registrations whose paths are already missing,
and preserves branches.
Workspace-local files, including
.BR .forest-workspace.toml ,
are reported and preserved; only an empty workspace directory with no active
child workspaces is removed.
Use
.B delete
to remove the workspace and its local files.
.IP
Naming checkouts leaves other worktrees in place:
.B remove stacked api@part-2
removes only that named checkout, and
.B api
means only the primary checkout.
Repeating a partially completed removal is safe.
.TP
.BI completions " shell"
Generate dynamic shell completion setup for
.BR bash ,
.BR elvish ,
.BR fish ,
.BR powershell ,
or
.BR zsh .
This command does not require a Forest configuration.
.PP
Forest serializes
.BR create ,
.BR add ,
.BR rename ,
.BR archive ,
.BR unarchive ,
.BR delete ,
.BR clean ,
and
.B remove
mutations for a configuration so they cannot race one another.
.SH SHELL COMPLETION
Evaluate the generated setup from the shell startup file rather than saving
its output, because the completion protocol is version-dependent.
For Bash or Zsh:
.PP
.EX
source <(git-forest completions bash)
.EE
.PP
Use
.B zsh
instead of
.B bash
for Zsh.
For Fish:
.PP
.EX
git-forest completions fish | source
.EE
.PP
For Elvish:
.PP
.EX
eval (git-forest completions elvish | slurp)
.EE
.PP
For PowerShell:
.PP
.EX
git-forest completions powershell | Out-String | Invoke-Expression
.EE
.PP
Bash, Zsh, and Fish complete both
.B git-forest
and
.BR "git forest" .
Dynamic candidates include configured repositories, existing workspaces, and
workspace checkouts where appropriate.
Completion honors normal configuration discovery,
.BR FOREST_CONFIG ,
and
.BR \-\-config .
It never contacts remotes or mutates worktrees.
Invalid or missing configuration produces no configuration-dependent
candidates.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Forest reads a
.B .forest.toml
file:
.PP
.EX
version = 1

[repositories]
root = "src"
remote = "git@github.com:example/{name}.git"
members = [
  "api",
  "operator",
]

[workspaces]
root = "src/.workspaces"
branch = "user/{checkout}"
.EE
.PP
The file is the one named by
.BR \-\-config ,
then the one named by
.BR FOREST_CONFIG ,
then the first
.B .forest.toml
found by walking from the current directory to the filesystem root.
All paths in it are relative to the directory containing it, including
relative local remotes.
.TP
.B repositories.root
The directory containing the canonical clones.
.TP
.B repositories.members
The repositories.
Each member is both its command-line name and its directory beneath the root.
.TP
.B repositories.remote
The clone URL template, which must contain
.BR {name} .
It is optional;
.B setup
requires it only when a canonical clone is missing.
.TP
.B workspaces.root
The directory containing workspaces.
Forest reserves
.B .archive
directly beneath it for archived workspace contents.
.TP
.B workspaces.branch
The branch template, which must contain
.B {workspace}
or
.BR {checkout} .
.B {workspace}
is the workspace name.
.B {checkout}
is the slot of a named checkout and the workspace name for a primary checkout.
.PP
Unknown placeholders, duplicate members, absolute roots, and unsupported
configuration versions are rejected.
.PP
Workspace names and checkout slots must match
.BR [A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]* .
The names "." and ".." are not valid.
A single request may not contain checkout identifiers that differ only by ASCII
case because they alias on case-insensitive filesystems.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B FOREST_CONFIG
Path to the configuration file.
It has lower precedence than
.BR \-\-config .
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable colored human-readable output.
.TP
.B PATH
Used to find Git.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including fully idempotent operations.
.TP
.B 1
An operational conflict or a Git or filesystem failure.
.TP
.B 2
A usage, input, or configuration error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git (1),
.BR git-worktree (1)
